    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe feature is wired through idiomatically overall \u2014 the persisted-at-creation approach mirrors the existing `countNamesakes`/`customerCode` pattern (computed once, stored, \"reflects state at creation time\"), `computeBulkSignupWarning` parallels `countNamesakes` closely, the `format*` mapper method follows the established prefix convention, and the entity/schema/OpenAPI additions all sit where their neighbors do. One concrete structural issue:\n\n- **Redundant adjacent `if (owner.isNew())` block** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:284-289`). The new write sits in its own guard immediately following the identical `assertDailyRegistrationLimit` guard:\n\n  ```java\n  if (owner.isNew()) {\n      assertDailyRegistrationLimit(owner);\n  }\n  if (owner.isNew()) {\n      owner.setBulkSignupWarning(computeBulkSignupWarning(owner));\n  }\n  ```\n\n  The other `isNew()` guards in this method each carry a *distinct* extra condition (`&& !sharesHousehold`, `&& getCustomerCode() == null`, `&& getNamesakeCount() == null`), so keeping them separate is justified. These two have the exact same condition and are adjacent, so the duplication isn't paying for anything. Fold the new line into the existing block:\n\n  ```java\n  if (owner.isNew()) {\n      assertDailyRegistrationLimit(owner);\n      owner.setBulkSignupWarning(computeBulkSignupWarning(owner));\n  }\n  ```\n\n  The doc comment on `computeBulkSignupWarning` even ties it to \"the daily-limit rule,\" which reinforces that the two belong in the same creation-time block.",
